WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:05.070
Special envoy Kerry, the cyclone we're seeing
in India is really hitting home the message

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:09.080
that more of those storms in the future are
a global threat.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:14.840
At the same time, the US is proud of having
gathered fifty five percent of global GDP

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.820
at that virtual summit.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.660
Yet Germany insists on exceptions.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.070
It doesn't want to leave coal till 2038.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.660
The US is continuing fracking.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:30.340
So how do you want to convince poorer countries
to do their part in the climate effort?

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:38.239
Well, it's critical that the 20 biggest emitters
in the world of which we are one and the EU

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:44.309
counts as a whole as one, and India and China
and others, we have to step up.

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:47.729
All of us have to lead here by example.

00:00:47.729 --> 00:00:56.670
And that means we need to be achieving significant
reduction goals, not just by 2050, but over

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.920
the next 10 years.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:05.851
If we 20 nations that are the largest economies
of the world don't mitigate- meaning, reduce

00:01:05.851 --> 00:01:12.880
our emissions during 2020 to 2030- then we
would be responsible for denying the rest

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.740
of the world the ability to hold the temperature
well below two degrees or at one point five.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.170
So that's our imperative.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:29.240
That's what we're trying to do now, hopefully
achieve through climate diplomacy over the

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.320
course of the next months before we go to
Glasgow.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.090
So in Glasgow, the less developed world will
be able to know that we've got the hundred

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:42.189
billion dollars we pulled together that has
been promised and that we are doing our part

00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:44.780
to keep one point five degrees alive.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:50.359
Now, reducing emissions is the one thing - at
the same time, the Earth's lung's under threat.

00:01:50.359 --> 00:01:55.799
Brazilian President Bolsonaro was also around
that table pledging that he would reduce illegal

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:57.740
logging, stop it till 2030.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:03.100
At the same time, the Amazon rainforest is
seeing a 10-year high in its decrease.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.640
How is the United States going to apply pressure
that those pledges are also met?

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:13.170
Well, we're working with President Bolsonaro
and with his team.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.660
We've had several conversations.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.470
We were certainly encouraged that he talked
about stopping the deforestation.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:25.010
But we believe, obviously, as do many scientists
and others in the world who are experts on

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.840
the Amazon, that we can't wait till 2030.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.510
We have to move much sooner than that.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:37.090
But we have to work with the country, the
sovereign nation that has control over its

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:41.880
own land and reach some kind of an agreement
if we possibly can.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:48.569
Obviously, there will be a need to have some
kind of enforcement that's credible and that

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:53.070
we have a different approach to this than
we've had previously.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:57.030
There's no guarantee we can get there, but
we're certainly going to try together with

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.099
other nations that are deeply concerned about
this.

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:05.060
Now, you've just met all major political actors
ahead of the September elections, the end

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.610
of the Merkel era here in Germany.

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:11.200
How would you feel about the government going
green?

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.220
Looking at the polling, the Greens could actually
have for the first time the chance to form

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:16.470
a government here.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:21.910
How would you feel about a Green government
at the helm of Europe's largest economy going

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.160
into that crucial climate summit in November?

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.060
Well, it's up to the people of Germany to
decide, you know, it's not up to me and how

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.689
I feel or President Biden feels about it.

00:03:32.689 --> 00:03:37.629
We certainly support the policy of addressing
climate crisis.

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:46.190
And obviously the Greens are very seized by
and engaged in that particular issue.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:50.140
But this is for the campaign ahead, not for
us.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.739
And unlike Russia, we're not going to involve
ourselves in other people's elections.

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:59.250
But you also discussed with the Pope that
he should probably participate in that climate

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.250
summit.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:04.200
Is that a sign that you politicians will no
longer be able to solve it alone?

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.200
No.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.430
It's a sign that a head of state member, he's
also a head of state,

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.329
...not a very big one though, but...

00:04:10.329 --> 00:04:11.329
Not a very big one.

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:17.830
But it speaks volumes that even as a small
one, that is a very, very powerful moral leader

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:24.540
in the world and the leader of more than just
the one point one billion Catholics, but a

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:30.010
very powerful voice who wrote a remarkable
document, Laudato si.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:39.150
I think he can help invigorate the folks who
are gathered there and have a message for

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.830
the world about the importance of what is
happening there.

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:47.480
But there'll be many leaders there who will
also join in that effort.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:53.730
This is the world's most important moment
with respect to multilateralism and a global

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:58.810
effort to solve a major international challenge.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:07.250
And if we don't get it right in Glasgow, then
we will be leaving certain parts of the world

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:14.050
in chaos and disorder and a great pain over
years, over the ensuing years, the less developed

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:22.960
world has not caused this problem, and they
are patiently waiting for the developed world

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.660
to step up and exercise their responsibility.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:32.890
And young people are asking adults to behave
like adults and get the job done.

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:34.620
So that's what's at stake.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.160
And I think having...

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:41.900
I'm delighted to hear that His Holiness wants
to come and wants to do this, and I think

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:43.580
his voice will be very important.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:48.690
Now, you yourself have cited nine years left
to go to avoid no longer being able to meet

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.990
the one point five degree aim.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.250
At the same time, you want to rely on largely
uninvented technology.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.210
Are you simply being optimistic?

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.270
Well, let me answer two parts of that.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:05.270
First of all, it's a mistake just to kind
of have a finite number of nine years, etc.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:11.790
Scientists gave us a broad range saying about
12 years left to make the most important decisions

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:15.510
to avoid the worst consequences of climate.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:20.260
But nobody can be that precise with respect
the environment about one year or two years.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:26.400
The idea is we have a very short period of
time and we have to ...we have to make those

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:27.400
decisions.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:34.220
Now, what I said about invention with respect
to it, the IEA, the International Energy Agency,

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:41.390
has said that forty two to forty six critical
technologies are not yet capable of being

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:44.160
brought to scale and put into the marketplace.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.160
That's what I was saying.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.000
It's not that we haven't invented some of
these things.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.710
It is that they're not at a scale where they
are commercially viable yet.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:59.840
And what we need to do is get green hydrogen
or battery storage or storage of some kind

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.830
and other things, able to be able to be brought
to the marketplace at scale.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:09.010
That's when it becomes a really viable opportunity.

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:16.060
But certainly with solar and with wind and
hydro and geothermal, we have a set of options

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:21.240
that get us very, very close in many, many
places where those are available.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:28.930
And we can do... a lot of nations are already
coming extremely close just with renewable

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.730
to being able to provide energy security.

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:36.220
I'm confident we can do this if we put our
mind to it.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:42.980
And very large sums of money are now beginning
to move from the private sector into investment

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.220
in the sustainable alternative renewable development
field.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:54.430
So I think, you know, I'm confident and optimistic
about our ability to get this done, providing

00:07:54.430 --> 00:08:01.310
we pay close attention not just to 30 years
from now with a big target, but with a very

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:08.820
specific set of plans for how we will reduce
sufficiently over the next 10 years, 2020

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.740
to 2030 is the key decade of decision.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:18.150
And that's what we'll decide a significant
part of all of our future of the planet's

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.150
future.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:20.150
Just one final question.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:24.880
When such close allies get together, surely
you have to talk about the situation in the

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:26.760
Middle East right now.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:31.200
How much longer will the United States block
a resolution calling for an end to the fighting

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:32.320
in the United Nations?

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.930
Look, I'm not ducking your question, but it's
not my daily work right now.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:43.400
I'm the climate envoy and President Biden
and Secretary Blinken are speaking to this

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:46.750
issue, and I'll leave it to them to do so.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.201
Special Envoy for Climate John Kerry, thank
you very much for talking to Deutsche Welle.

00:08:49.201 --> 00:08:50.201
Thank you very much.

00:08:50.201 --> 00:08:50.203
Appreciate it.

